","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuliaLang/julia/issues/55760/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuliaLang/julia/issues/55760/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null},"comment":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuliaLang/julia/issues/comments/2348418739","html_url":"https://github.com/JuliaLang/julia/pull/55760#issuecomment-2348418739","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuliaLang/julia/issues/55760","id":2348418739,"node_id":"IC_kwDOABkWpM6L-gaz","user":{"login":"aravindh-krishnamoorthy","id":22256003,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjIyMjU2MDAz","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/22256003?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/aravindh-krishnamoorthy","html_url":"https://github.com/aravindh-krishnamoorthy","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aravindh-krishnamoorthy/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aravindh-krishnamoorthy/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aravindh-krishnamoorthy/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aravindh-krishnamoorthy/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aravindh-krishnamoorthy/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aravindh-krishnamoorthy/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aravindh-krishnamoorthy/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aravindh-krishnamoorthy/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aravindh-krishnamoorthy/received_events","type":"User","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2024-09-13T09:00:48Z","updated_at":"2024-09-13T09:00:48Z","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","body":"> The "promise" of `ldiv!` is not to be fast, but to operate in-place. The usage pattern is like this:
> 
> ```julia
> A = factorize(rand(3,3)) # for instance B = rand(3, 5) ldiv!(A, B)
> # continue to work with B
> ```
> 
> If this wasn't in-place, you would need `C = ldiv!(A, B)`, to have a handle for the result, which users don't expect. So they would miss the result, and believe the result is stored in `B`. It does happen already that in-place methods are slower than allocating ones: e.g. that is the case for `mul!` vs. `*` with different BLAS-eltypes.

Thank you, @dkarrasch. I fully agree that in the final version, `B` must be in-place. To this end, I will evaluate various options of in-place transposition and filling, including the proposal by @martinholters, and other options in `generic_matmul`.

I feel that it may be best to use LAPACK for `rdiv!` and `ldiv!` when the type is `BlasComplex` or `BlasFloat`. Would you agree with this assessment?


